  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper-level low continues to be positioned in central-western\nTexas driving storm motion here in southern Arizona. Isolated \nto scattered showers and thunderstorms continue to pop up areas \neast of Tucson. Substantial moisture remains in place today with\nsurface dewpoints in the mid 50s to upper 60s and satellite \nderived PWATs between 1.5 to 1.8 inches. CAMs are still having a\nhard time capturing what is going to happen today, showing \nwidely varying outcomes with each run of the models. In general,\nthunderstorms seem to be focused to areas east and south of \nTucson, initiating off the terrain and moving to the south at 5 \nto 10 mph. The main concern associated with storms today is \nheavy downpours leading to nuisance ponding and flooding and \nflash flooding associated with stronger storm. While wind is not\na huge concern today there may still be strong to gusty outflow\nwinds.\n\nOver the next few days, the low over western TX will morph into\nan inverted trough as high pressure currently to our west will\ngradually move into southern UT and CO by Monday. The trough\nwill push into eastern AZ starting Monday into northern Arizona\nby Tuesday as high pressure moves into the central U.S. This \nwill help bring in more moisture into the area and slow \nsoutherly flow leading to our next up day. The Weather \nPrediction Center has placed portions of AZ into slight risk \nthrough Tuesday. Sunday and Monday there are still chances for \ndaily thunderstorms each day with their own nuances.",
